{"id":"find-the-index-of-the-first-occurrence","title":"Find the Index of the First Occurrence in a String","difficulty":"Easy","pattern":"Strings","tags":["Two Pointers","String","String Matching"],"companies":{"google":2,"apple":3,"meta":2,"amazon":3,"microsoft":3},"description":"Given two strings `haystack` and `needle`, return the index of the first position in `haystack` where `needle` begins, or `-1` if `needle` does not occur. This is the classic `strStr` / `indexOf` routine.\n\nYou may not call the language's built-in substring search; the point is to implement it.","examples":[{"input":"haystack = \"sadbutsad\", needle = \"sad\"","output":"0","explanation":"sad occurs at 0 and 6; the first is 0."},{"input":"haystack = \"leetcode\", needle = \"leeto\"","output":"-1"},{"input":"haystack = \"mississippi\", needle = \"issip\"","output":"4","explanation":"The earlier partial match at index 1 (issis) fails on the fifth character."}],"constraints":["1 <= haystack.length, needle.length <= 10^4","Both strings consist of lowercase English letters"],"hints":["The direct approach tries every start index i and compares needle against haystack[i:i+m]. Worst case O(n*m) but fine for small inputs.","To guarantee O(n+m), use KMP: precompute, for each prefix of needle, the length of its longest proper prefix that is also a suffix (the failure table).","While scanning the haystack, on a mismatch fall back to the failure table instead of restarting at the next haystack index; the haystack pointer never moves backwards."],"function":"strStr","signature":"def strStr(self, haystack: str, needle: str) -> int:","arg_types":{"haystack":"str","needle":"str"},"return_type":"int","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"haystack":"sadbutsad","needle":"sad"},"output":0},{"input":{"haystack":"leetcode","needle":"leeto"},"output":-1},{"input":{"haystack":"mississippi","needle":"issip"},"output":4},{"input":{"haystack":"hello","needle":"ll"},"output":2},{"input":{"haystack":"aaaaa","needle":"bba"},"output":-1},{"input":{"haystack":"a","needle":"a"},"output":0},{"input":{"haystack":"abc","needle":"c"},"output":2},{"input":{"haystack":"ab","needle":"abc"},"output":-1},{"input":{"haystack":"aabaaabaaac","needle":"aabaaac"},"output":4},{"input":{"haystack":"","needle":""},"output":3000},{"input":{"haystack":"","needle":""},"output":-1},{"input":{"haystack":"","needle":""},"output":3000}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def strStr(self, haystack: str, needle: str) -> int:\n        n, m = len(haystack), len(needle)\n        if m == 0:\n            return 0\n        # failure[i] = length of the longest proper prefix of needle[:i+1] that is also its suffix\n        failure = [0] * m\n        k = 0\n        for i in range(1, m):\n            while k and needle[i] != needle[k]:\n                k = failure[k - 1]\n            if needle[i] == needle[k]:\n                k += 1\n            failure[i] = k\n        # scan the haystack; j is how many needle chars are currently matched\n        j = 0\n        for i, ch in enumerate(haystack):\n            while j and ch != needle[j]:\n                j = failure[j - 1]\n            if ch == needle[j]:\n                j += 1\n                if j == m:\n                    return i - m + 1\n        return -1\n","approach":"**Key insight (KMP): after a mismatch, the characters you just matched tell you exactly how far the pattern can slide, so the text pointer never goes back.** Precompute `failure[i]`, the length of the longest proper prefix of `needle[:i+1]` that is also a suffix of it. When the match of length `j` breaks, the best next alignment keeps the first `failure[j-1]` characters matched. The same loop structure builds the table (pattern against itself) and runs the search (pattern against text).\n\nEach step either advances the text pointer or shrinks `j`, and `j` grows at most once per text character, so the search is O(n) after an O(m) preprocessing pass: O(n + m) total with O(m) space.\n\nThe naive sliding comparison is O((n - m + 1) * m) in the worst case (text `aaa...a`, pattern `aa...ab`) but is the right first answer in an interview; implement it, then explain where it degrades and offer KMP. In Python, slicing `haystack[i:i+m] == needle` is fast in practice because the comparison runs in C.\n\n**Common mistakes:** off-by-one in the return index (`i - m + 1`); forgetting to fall back repeatedly (the inner `while`, not a single `if`); comparing `needle[j]` after `j` already equals `m`; handling an empty needle.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Rabin-Karp (rolling hash) for searching many patterns of the same length or for 2-D matching; Boyer-Moore for long alphabets and long patterns (sublinear on average); Aho-Corasick for many patterns at once. Also ask what a production `indexOf` does: most libraries use a tuned two-way or Boyer-Moore-Horspool variant rather than plain KMP.","complexity":{"time":"O(n + m)","space":"O(m)"},"similar":["repeated-substring-pattern","longest-common-prefix","shortest-palindrome"],"number":183,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def strStr(self, haystack: str, needle: str) -> int:\n        pass\n"}